   ("callout", "The compounding trap", "em compounds through nesting. If a list has font-size 0.9em and it sits inside another element at 0.9em, the text shrinks twice, and a third level shrinks again. This cascade is the main reason people prefer rem for global sizing, because rem always measures from the root and never compounds."),
   ("p", "So a practical split is: use rem for global, predictable sizing like base font size, headings and layout spacing, and use em for local sizing that should track a specific element, like the internal padding of a component. That gives you predictability where you want it and proportional scaling where it helps."),
